.TH GOD 1
.SH NAME
god \- TempleOS God: words, songs and Bible passages from user-gated entropy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B temple/god
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I God
is a port of the TempleOS
.B /Adam/God
suite (HolySpirit, GodWord, GodSong, GodBiblePassage).
It runs under
.IR wm (1).
.PP
All randomness is drawn from a bit fifo that is fed only by the
.I entropy ceremony.
Whenever a pick needs more bits than the fifo holds, a modal panel
replaces the window contents, showing two live hexadecimal readouts \-
.B Timer
(a free-running microsecond clock) and
.B Latch
(the timestamp of the most recent keyboard or mouse event) \-
the message
.RB \(lqPress " OKAY " "to generate a random num from a timer\(rq,"
and an
.B OKAY
button.
Dismissing the panel latches the timestamp of the user's own click;
its low 4 bits are discarded and the next 24 bits are fed to the fifo.
The user's act of choosing when to click is the randomness,
as in the original.
.PP
The buttons are:
.TP
.B God Word
Draw 17 bits and print the selected word from the vocabulary list.
.TP
.B God Song
Show the song settings form (complexity
.BR Simple / Normal / Complex ,
.B Rests
and
.B Six Eight
checkboxes, and an octave field, clamped to 1\-7).
Two independent two-measure strings are generated,
each from a freshly flushed fifo \- so the form and the ceremony
run twice \- and the result is printed and played as
AABB through
.IR tone (2).
Notes span two octaves (G, A, B in the base octave and C\-G above it),
with rests only when
.B Rests
is checked.
In the form, Escape accepts, as TempleOS
.B ESC
does.
.TP
.B Bible passage
Draw 21 bits, print the
.RI \(lq "Book chapter:verse" \(rq
citation resolved from the 66-book line table of the original,
a blank line, then 20 lines of the King James text.
.TP
.B keyboard
.B Esc
or
.B q
quits; in the entropy panel,
.B Esc
or
.B Enter
acts as OKAY.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /lib/temple/vocab.txt
Word list, the original's
.B /Adam/God/Vocab.DD
verbatim.
.TP
.B /lib/temple/Bible.TXT
King James Bible text addressed by the book line table.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/temple/god.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR temple (1),
.IR tone (2),
.IR wm (1)
.SH BUGS
The original emits each song as a clickable DolDoc song object;
here the song string is printed and played immediately.
.IR tone (2)
has no tuplet duration, so eighth-note triplets are approximated
by dotted sixteenths (the printed string keeps the TempleOS
.B et
notation).
GodDoodle is not ported.
